; ❯ how I can level up beyound this challenge, for leveling up my skills?
    1. **Finish [pwn.college](https://pwn.college/).** Complete the rest of this module.
    2. **Do the [PortSwigger Academy labs](https://portswigger.net/web-security/os-command-injection)**, especially the blind command injection ones. You won't see output there, so you have to detect it from timing or from requests the server makes to you.
    3. **Read real source code.** Search [GitHub](https://github.com/search?type=code) for `shell=True`, `os.system(` and `exec(` alongside request input, and ask yourself whether each one is exploitable. That's how real bugs get found.
    4. **Learn the shell properly.** Read the ["Shell Command Language" chapter of the POSIX spec](https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html) on quoting, expansion and word splitting. Most filter bypasses follow from it.
    5. **Study the fixes too.** Use argument lists instead of a shell string, use [`shlex.quote`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html#shlex.quote) when you can't avoid the shell, and run with least privilege. Knowing why a fix works shows you where incomplete fixes break.
    6. **Play real CTFs on [ctftime.org](https://ctftime.org/)**, then read the writeups for challenges you didn't solve.
    7. **Look at CVEs.** Search ["command injection CVE"](https://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvekey.cgi?keyword=command+injection) and read the patch diffs, since IoT and router firmware is full of these bugs.

; Read-made payload cuted lists
    - [**PayloadsAllTheThings**](https://github.com/swisskyrepo/PayloadsAllTheThings): organized by bug class, with explanations. Best for learning.
    - [**SecLists**](https://github.com/danielmiessler/SecLists) (danielmiessler, GitHub): large raw wordlists for fuzzing, including a command injection set under `Fuzzing/`.
    - [**HackTricks**](https://book.hacktricks.wiki/): technique notes with payloads, including filter bypasses.
    - **PortSwigger cheat sheets:** [XSS](https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting/cheat-sheet) and [SQLi](https://portswigger.net/web-security/sql-injection/cheat-sheet) especially, kept up to date.
    - [**GTFOBins**](https://gtfobins.github.io/) / [**LOLBAS**](https://lolbas-project.github.io/): abusing Linux and Windows binaries after you get in.
    - ctftime.org : CTF writeups on ctftime.org: search "command injection" and read how people beat filters.


; Shellcode DB
    ## Polyglots and file-format tricks
    - **Ange Albertini ([corkami](https://github.com/corkami))**: files that are valid as several formats at once, with gorgeous diagrams.
    - **[PoC||GTFO](https://pocorgtfo.hacke.rs/)**: the journal is literally a polyglot (the PDF is also a ZIP, an image, and so on). The whole thing is payload art.
    - **[Phrack](https://phrack.org/)** for classic exploitation craft.

    ## Binary / shellcode
    - **[shell-storm.org shellcode database](https://shell-storm.org/shellcode/)** and [Exploit-DB's shellcode section](https://www.exploit-db.com/shellcodes).
    - **["A Whirlwind Tutorial on Creating Really Teensy ELF Executables"](https://www.muppetlabs.com/~breadbox/software/tiny/teensy.html)** (muppetlabs): a classic of minimalism.
    - **Demoscene 256-byte and 4K intros** ([pouet.net](https://www.pouet.net/)): the same mindset, just not offensive.

    ## Ongoing feed
    - Writeups on **[CTFtime.org](https://ctftime.org/writeups)**: filter by the web or pwn category and read the top-rated ones.
    - Follow the researchers above on X, Mastodon or Bluesky. A lot of the best payloads are first posted there as one-line puzzles.
